 tensor_R J with Q; the image I of J is a nonzero finitely generated R-submodule of Q. Clearing denominators gives a nonzero finitely generated ideal dI of R. Every nonzero q in Q has form p^k u for integer k and unit u in R. For a finite set of nonzero generators of an ideal, choose the smallest such exponent k; all generators are in p^k R and one is p^k times a unit, so the ideal equals p^k R. Thus dI is principal and J is free of rank one. Choose a generator a. Then a e_i form an R-basis of M_R.\\n\\nOn this basis, x(ae_i)=(lambda-i+1)ae_(i-1), y(ae_i)=(i+1)ae_(i+1), and h(ae_i)=(lambda-2i)ae_i. Reducing modulo p gives the standard module on the reduced basis. Localization and reduction identify M_R/pM_R with M tensor_Z F_p: a Z-basis of M yields the same reduced basis and the same reduced integral action matrices. Extending scalars from F_p to K gives an L(K)-module isomorphism with the standard reduced module over K.\\n\\nIn that module, for r<=lambda<p, r! is invertible modulo p, so X_r and Y_r are scalar multiples of powers of x and y. The matrix-unit identities remain valid modulo p, since binom(lambda,j) is nonzero modulo p. Any subspace W invariant under x,y,h is consequently invariant under every E_i,j. Given a nonzero vector w=sum_i c_i e_i in W, select i with c_i nonzero. Then E_ji w=c_i e_j for every j, so all basis vectors belong to W and W is the whole module. The module has dimension lambda+1>0. This includes lambda=0; no step requires p>2 or distinct reduced h-weights.\\\",\\\"source_obligations\\\":\\\"No general literature theorem is imported. The divided-power formulas follow by iteration from the supplied sl2 relations and highest-weight equations. Matrix-unit identities and all R-module arguments are derived explicitly above. The characteristic-zero basis and dimension, the admissible lattice properties, and action setup are supplied by the frozen context and hypotheses.\\\",\\\"edge_cases\\\":\\\"lambda=0 gives the one-dimensional module and identity matrix unit. p=2 is included, with lambda=0 or 1 and no division by 2. M is arbitrary admissible, not restricted to a particular lattice. Any field K of characteristic p follows by scalar extension from F_p.\\\"}\"}","status":"completed"}}

